    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d62">

                <pb facs="0190" n="94" sameAs="frus:frus1926v02/pg94"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">800.51 W 89France/315: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Ambassador in France</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                        >Herrick</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic">to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Paraphrase]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Paris</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1926-06-04T11:00:00+01:00"><hi rendition="#italic">June 4, 1926—11
                                a.m.</hi></date><lb/>[Received June 4—8:55 a.m.]</dateline></opener>
                <p>224. In a telephone conversation on the evening of June 3, Bérenger stated that
                    his task of obtaining ratification of the French debt agreement would be
                    facilitated if the United States Treasury Department <pb facs="0191" n="95"
                        xml:id="pg95"/> would raise the embargo against the firm of Boue
                        Soeurs<anchor n="6" xml:id="d62-f1"/> as a mark of good will. This firm
                    would seem to have been punished sufficiently for the faults which it may have
                    committed heretofore, and as apparently it has very powerful connections, I hope
                    that Mr. Mellon<anchor n="7" xml:id="d62-f2"/> may see his way clear to take
                    action such as suggested by M. Bérenger. This might furthermore deter the French
                    press from outbursts against our customs representatives abroad for their
                    alleged inquisitorial methods.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Herrick</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
            <note corresp="#d62-f1" n="6" xml:id="d62-f1-content">
                <p>A Treasury Department order of Aug. 31, 1925, as amended Sept. 16, 1925,
                    prohibiting, in accordance with section 510 of the tariff act of 1922, the
                    importation and delivery of merchandise manufactured by or for the account of
                    Boue Soeurs, Ltd., of Paris, for refusal of said firm to submit certain of its
                    books and records for inspection.</p>
            </note>
            <note corresp="#d62-f2" n="7" xml:id="d62-f2-content">
                <p>Andrew Mellon, Secretary of the Treasury and Chairman of the World War Foreign
                    Debt Commission.</p>
            </note>
        </listAnnotation>
    </standOff>
